--- WHY THERE IS NO SWEEP -----------------------------------------------------
sky130A HAS NO PARASITIC COEFFICIENT TABLE in this repo.
`.claude/skills/parasitic-estimation/script/estimate_parasitics.py` sets
`PDK_TABLES = {}`, and its module docstring says this is deliberate:

    "DELIBERATELY NOT RESTORED -- `PDK_TABLES` ... Those are measured process
     numbers; inventing them would produce plausible, wrong nf decisions. It is
     left EMPTY on purpose so `sweep_fingers.check_nf()` takes its own
     already-written 'no parasitic coefficient table for pdk=...' branch and the
     caller reports the nf as never measured, rather than silently shaping
     against fabricated physics."

Confirmed by running the sweep exactly as SKILL.md specifies rather than by
reading the source alone:

    sweep_fingers.py: error: argument --pdk: invalid choice: 'sky130A'
                      (choose from )

The empty `choices=sorted(PDK_TABLES)` means NO PDK is currently selectable, so
this is not a sky130A-specific gap -- device-shaper's Step 2 cannot run for any
process until a table is added. NO RULE OF THUMB WAS SUBSTITUTED. There is no
sweep, no spread, no rel_drop and no selected Nf in this file because none was
measured, and an unmeasured nf must not be presented as a chosen one.

CONSEQUENCE, stated plainly for layout: nf=1 on all six MOS devices is a
DEFAULT CARRIED THROUGH, not a decision. Two of them are wide by any standard --
XN2 and XN3 at w=60 um -- and a 60 um single-finger device has high gate
resistance and a large drain junction sitting directly on the tank node. Folding
them would very likely help. Nobody has measured by how much, or at what Nf the
curve turns, and this skill exists precisely because that curve is not monotonic
and cannot be guessed.

--- STEP 1 -- DEVICE SELECTION (RAN, and worked) ------------------------------
select_shape_devices.py read circuit_decomposition.yaml's parasitic_sensitivity
section and selected 4 devices from the `severity: high` entries:

    SWEPT (would have been):  XN0, XN1, XN2, XN3
    PINNED at netlist nf:     XN4=nf1, XN5=nf1
    specs at risk:            OscFreq, OutputPower, TuningRange
    CLAMPED:                  none (nothing ran)

It correctly refused to turn prose into devices: the top-ranked entry's `ref` is
the tank-node description "['voutp', 'voutn tank nodes -- XN0', 'XN1', ...]" and
only the real MOS instance names inside it were taken. XC0/XC1 (MiM caps) were
excluded as non-MOS.

NOTE FROM STEP 1 -- A HIGH-SEVERITY ENTRY WITH NO MOS DEVICE TO FOLD:
    ['XL0', 'XL1']  severity high  threatens OscFreq, OutputPower
  The two spiral inductors. They are `vco_spiral_ind` subckt instances holding
  only L/R/C -- no MOS, no w/l, tie group TG5 status not_tunable. THIS SKILL HAD
  NO LEVER FOR THEM AT ALL, and they are the largest parasitic exposure in the
  design: two ~196 x 213 um coils whose mutual coupling, real substrate
  capacitance and true Q are not in the lumped pi-model. The netlist's own
  header warns the model is optimistic at ~0.75x f_SR. Only an EM-extracted
  coil model answers this; no finger count can.

WARNING FROM STEP 1: circuit_decomposition.yaml carries `confirmed_by_user:
false`. The severity ranking that chose these devices is therefore PROVISIONAL
-- schematic-agent had no interactive channel to close it. See its report.

